1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,952
Section zero showed you this exact moment: Dana and Sam each privately picked a

2
00:00:04,952 --> 00:00:09,786
winner before any data existed, both confident, both wrong about which one actually

3
00:00:09,786 --> 00:00:10,019
won.

4
00:00:10,019 --> 00:00:14,679
This lecture explains why it happens, mechanically, so it stops feeling like bad

5
00:00:14,679 --> 00:00:18,115
luck and starts feeling like something you can plan around.

6
00:00:18,115 --> 00:00:21,261
Here's the claim, stated as sharply as I can state it.

7
00:00:21,261 --> 00:00:25,862
How confident someone is that a variant will win tells you almost nothing about

8
00:00:25,862 --> 00:00:27,318
whether it actually will.

9
00:00:27,318 --> 00:00:29,415
Not "somewhat less than you'd hope".

10
00:00:29,415 --> 00:00:30,406
Close to nothing.

11
00:00:30,406 --> 00:00:35,182
That's a hard claim to sit with if you've built a career on having good instincts,

12
00:00:35,182 --> 00:00:36,813
so let's not just assert it.

13
00:00:36,813 --> 00:00:41,473
What does it actually look like when you write confidence down before a test and

14
00:00:41,473 --> 00:00:43,395
compare it to what happens after?

15
00:00:43,945 --> 00:00:48,639
This is a realistic shape for what happens when you rate confidence before a test

16
00:00:48,639 --> 00:00:52,580
and rank results after it, and it's worth sitting with for a second.

17
00:00:52,580 --> 00:00:55,767
The variant the team loved most landed near the bottom.

18
00:00:55,767 --> 00:01:00,403
The one nobody believed in, generated almost as an afterthought to round out the

19
00:01:00,403 --> 00:01:01,562
batch, won outright.

20
00:01:01,562 --> 00:01:04,112
This isn't a rigged example to make a point.

21
00:01:04,112 --> 00:01:08,922
It's the ordinary, unremarkable shape of a real test, and if you've run more than a

22
00:01:08,922 --> 00:01:13,501
handful of these yourself, some version of this table probably matches your own

23
00:01:13,501 --> 00:01:13,964
results.

24
00:01:13,964 --> 00:01:18,253
Notice, too, that "safe and obvious" didn't protect the second row either.

25
00:01:18,253 --> 00:01:22,078
It landed squarely in the middle, neither embarrassing nor useful.

26
00:01:22,078 --> 00:01:25,729
Playing it safe doesn't buy you a mediocre-but-reliable result.

27
00:01:25,729 --> 00:01:30,597
It just buys you a guess with less conviction behind it, which is not the same thing

28
00:01:30,597 --> 00:01:31,640
as a better guess.

29
00:01:32,190 --> 00:01:35,491
Here's the actual mechanism, not just the observation.

30
00:01:35,491 --> 00:01:40,075
A variant wins because of how it interacts with the audience seeing it, the

31
00:01:40,075 --> 00:01:45,026
placement it lands in, the algorithm's delivery choices that day, and the mood of

32
00:01:45,026 --> 00:01:47,470
someone mid-scroll at that exact moment.

33
00:01:47,470 --> 00:01:52,421
Looking at a static image or a script gives you exactly one of those four inputs.

34
00:01:52,421 --> 00:01:56,333
Confidence built from one input out of four isn't weak evidence.

35
00:01:56,333 --> 00:02:01,162
It's evidence about a different question than the one you're actually trying to

36
00:02:01,162 --> 00:02:01,590
answer.

37
00:02:01,590 --> 00:02:06,540
Try this before your next real test, because it's a fast way to feel the scale of

38
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:07,274
the problem.

39
00:02:07,274 --> 00:02:12,347
Have two or three people rank the variants blind, without discussing it first, then

40
00:02:12,347 --> 00:02:16,503
compare their rankings to each other before anyone looks at results.

41
00:02:16,503 --> 00:02:19,926
Most of the time, they won't even agree with each other.

42
00:02:19,926 --> 00:02:24,877
If two experienced people can't agree on the ranking, expecting either of them to

43
00:02:24,877 --> 00:02:27,261
agree with reality was never realistic.

44
00:02:27,811 --> 00:02:32,813
Here's how this plays out organisationally, and it's worth naming because it's not

45
00:02:32,813 --> 00:02:35,253
really about any one person's judgement.

46
00:02:35,253 --> 00:02:40,315
In a room with no data yet, the most confident voice fills the vacuum, and the most

47
00:02:40,315 --> 00:02:44,829
confident voice usually belongs to whoever's most senior or most invested.

48
00:02:44,829 --> 00:02:49,953
That opinion gets the budget by default, not through a deliberate decision that it's

49
00:02:49,953 --> 00:02:51,417
more likely to be right.

50
00:02:51,417 --> 00:02:56,358
Marketers have a blunt name for this: the HiPPO effect, the highest paid person's

51
00:02:56,358 --> 00:02:59,774
opinion, winning not on accuracy but on volume and rank.

52
00:02:59,774 --> 00:03:02,397
It's not a character flaw in senior people.

53
00:03:02,397 --> 00:03:07,155
It's what happens by default when confidence is the only currency in the room.

54
00:03:07,155 --> 00:03:09,046
The fix isn't a better opinion.

55
00:03:09,046 --> 00:03:14,170
It's removing opinion from the decision entirely, which is exactly what a kill rule,

56
00:03:14,170 --> 00:03:16,854
decided before anyone's seen a result, does.

57
00:03:16,854 --> 00:03:21,063
There's a quieter cost to this pattern too, beyond the wasted budget.

58
00:03:21,063 --> 00:03:26,187
When the loudest guess always wins, the people with the least confident voice in the

59
00:03:26,187 --> 00:03:31,310
room, often the ones actually running the account day to day, learn to stop offering

60
00:03:31,310 --> 00:03:32,408
their read at all.

61
00:03:32,408 --> 00:03:36,129
You lose their signal permanently, not just on this one test.

62
00:03:36,679 --> 00:03:41,065
I don't want you to walk away thinking judgement is useless now, because that's not

63
00:03:41,065 --> 00:03:42,703
the argument and it isn't true.

64
00:03:42,703 --> 00:03:47,088
Taste has no predictive signal for ranking winners, and that's the specific, narrow

65
00:03:47,088 --> 00:03:48,251
claim of this lecture.

66
00:03:48,251 --> 00:03:52,478
It has plenty of signal for deciding what's on brand, catching a claim before it

67
00:03:52,478 --> 00:03:56,864
embarrasses the account, and writing a brief wide enough to cover angles a narrower

68
00:03:56,864 --> 00:03:58,132
brief would have missed.

69
00:03:58,132 --> 00:04:02,095
Those are real skills, and none of this course asks you to switch them off.

70
00:04:02,095 --> 00:04:04,684
The discipline is knowing which job you're doing.

71
00:04:04,684 --> 00:04:06,216
Judging the brief: your call.

72
00:04:06,216 --> 00:04:10,654
Judging which finished ad wins: not your call, not anyone's, until the test says so.

73
00:04:10,654 --> 00:04:14,987
If you find that distinction hard to hold onto in the moment, here's a rough test.

74
00:04:14,987 --> 00:04:19,214
If the question is "would I be comfortable this went out under our name", that's

75
00:04:19,214 --> 00:04:20,905
taste, and it's yours to answer.

76
00:04:20,905 --> 00:04:25,185
If the question is "which one will perform best", that's not a question taste can

77
00:04:25,185 --> 00:04:27,404
answer at all, no matter how it's phrased.

78
00:04:27,954 --> 00:04:32,809
Next lecture puts a number on all of this: exactly how many variants you need

79
00:04:32,809 --> 00:04:37,979
running before the tail from Section one-point-two reliably shows up, so "generate

80
00:04:37,979 --> 00:04:40,627
more" turns into a plan instead of a hope.

81
00:04:40,627 --> 00:04:45,798
Before that, write the one-line split down somewhere you'll actually see it again:

82
00:04:45,798 --> 00:04:49,455
which calls are yours, and which calls belong to the test.

83
00:04:49,455 --> 00:04:54,310
That sentence is the first filter Section 2.6's brief, artifact B01, is built

84
00:04:54,310 --> 00:04:58,156
around, so having it ready now is a head start, not homework.
